            "Antibiotics are the only drugs whose misuse harms people who never took them, because every unnecessary course breeds resistance that someone else will meet. Four questions come before any antibiotic. <strong>Is it even bacterial?</strong> Most sore throats, colds, coughs of a few days without fever and watery diarrhoeas are viral, and an antibiotic will not shorten them by a day — the patient with a three-day cough and no fever who asks for an antibiotic, or who 'always finds they work', is offered an explanation, symptomatic treatment and safety-netting, not a prescription. <strong>Do you need to kill or only stop growth?</strong> <strong>Bactericidal</strong> drugs (penicillins, cephalosporins, aminoglycosides, quinolones, metronidazole) kill the organism and are essential where the immune system cannot finish the job — meningitis, endocarditis, neutropenia; <strong>bacteriostatic</strong> drugs (tetracyclines, macrolides, sulphonamides, chloramphenicol) stop multiplication and rely on the host to clear the rest. <strong>Can the drug reach the site?</strong> A drug that does not cross into the brain, the bone, an abscess or the prostate is useless there however sensitive the organism looks on the report. <strong>Who is the patient?</strong> Allergy, pregnancy, breastfeeding, age, kidney and liver function, interactions and cost all change the choice. <strong>Penicillin</strong> works by blocking the building of the bacterial cell wall, a structure human cells do not have, which is why it kills bacteria without harming us and why its main danger is not toxicity but <strong>anaphylaxis</strong>.",
