          columns: ["Principle", "Detail", "Drugs to know"],
          rows: [
            ["<strong>Ask every woman of childbearing age</strong> before prescribing and again before dispensing", "Most drugs cross the placenta; the <strong>first trimester (weeks 3–8 of organogenesis)</strong> is the window for structural defects, later exposure affects growth, organ function and the newborn; untreated illness (epilepsy, hypertension, malaria, HIV, asthma, depression) also harms the baby, so the answer is rarely simply 'stop'.", "<strong>Teratogens and drugs to avoid</strong>: ACE inhibitors and ARBs (fetal kidneys), warfarin (first-trimester embryopathy and bleeding — heparin instead), sodium valproate (neural tube defects), isotretinoin and thalidomide, methotrexate and cytotoxics, tetracyclines (teeth and bone), aminoglycosides and streptomycin (deafness), misoprostol (abortion — except under obstetric protocol), NSAIDs in the third trimester (closure of the ductus, kidney failure), statins, lithium (heart defects), radioactive iodine, high-dose vitamin A, cotrimoxazole near term, chloramphenicol near term, live vaccines, primaquine, and most herbal preparations."],
            ["<strong>Prefer drugs with a record</strong>", "Choose the medicine with the longest record of safe use at the lowest effective dose for the shortest time; check every new drug in a pregnancy reference.", "Methyldopa, labetalol and nifedipine for hypertension; insulin (and metformin) for diabetes; paracetamol for pain; penicillins, cephalosporins, erythromycin and azithromycin for infection; artemether-lumefantrine or quinine with clindamycin for malaria according to trimester and guidance; heparins for anticoagulation; iron, folic acid 0.4 mg (5 mg before and during early pregnancy in women on antiepileptics or with a previous neural tube defect), IPTp-SP from 16 weeks, tetanus-diphtheria toxoid."],
            ["<strong>Breastfeeding — most medicines are compatible</strong>", "Only a small fraction of most drugs reaches the milk; weigh the infant's small exposure against the harm of stopping breastfeeding; take doses immediately after a feed or before the baby's longest sleep; watch the baby for drowsiness, poor feeding and rash.", "Safe choices: paracetamol and ibuprofen (the analgesics of choice after caesarean section or perineal repair), most antibiotics, antimalarial ACTs, antiretrovirals, insulin, levothyroxine, heparins and warfarin, methyldopa and nifedipine, inhaled drugs. <strong>Avoid or use with caution</strong>: codeine and tramadol (infant respiratory depression in ultra-rapid metabolisers), aspirin, tetracyclines, chloramphenicol, cytotoxics, lithium, amiodarone, radioactive iodine, ergotamine, high-dose oestrogen combined pills (suppress milk — progestogen-only methods are used), sedating antihistamines and benzodiazepines in high doses, and <strong>bromocriptine or cabergoline</strong>, which stop lactation and are used only when that is intended."],
          ],
